#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8880916-6af5-578d-965e-fd9d6f88b4a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349270} {:answer/id #uuid "4464d7b8-9772-5def-8606-4ad8529a8072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y(2-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349271} {:answer/id #uuid "d8be0ffd-3951-5730-88fb-afe685a3ecdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=-y(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349272} {:answer/id #uuid "58255768-8bc8-58d7-9589-322253ac8e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349273} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4b6a89-ec92-5a01-8642-f1e512c8a05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8880916-6af5-578d-965e-fd9d6f88b4a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349270} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d060ea3-1e32-50c4-bdf4-7688d9fcc126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbebd003-2374-5d75-bbd7-db61c097cd65" :question/math-academy-id "q-115931" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/4f9f16654bc92f01c9cda8308ab4679695ed7d5055a12f6963303d4071c95bea.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/0234179c0a2dfb514a921f19e6fdc7bcad022aa0368d122a7d2ecc0e5d27df73.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}(2-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(2-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d0b4c0f-1f2f-5205-bd35-4f842a9b9f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163539} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b65853-d5f0-5764-bbd2-245baa0b7da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163540} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9141c6-937b-5681-90df-ecfac7da1d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163541} {:answer/id #uuid "70658603-c848-5e65-ac3c-909347e3066d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163542} {:answer/id #uuid "7412d356-b039-5c7b-882a-ab2989724b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d0b4c0f-1f2f-5205-bd35-4f842a9b9f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163539} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69c49ed-e244-558d-8a7f-f50212e91626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90e07e12-e687-5234-b4a1-b563e2fcae03" :question/math-academy-id "q-116024" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/660feebe44e179b53be85d23c497e58de4774258b588a6f8ac785a0fd9c8fc08.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/a76856ea0a6b50b821ba6c39e6a87a5acf5aabd704454b4875540b3970f3d249.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}=-\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for all $y\\ne 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}=-\\frac{y+1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to ∓\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ac38a65-a187-54e3-92d9-1a95c96d36b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349277} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b8df4f-64f4-53c5-a1d5-824be7db6b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349278} {:answer/id #uuid "74f6150b-ebb6-59fa-aa62-0bd74bae70a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349279} {:answer/id #uuid "a94018d1-7bba-5224-be71-d6c40a0ec287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349280} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb147d5-bd44-5f2a-ac96-8461c8c1b811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bb147d5-bd44-5f2a-ac96-8461c8c1b811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349281} :answer-field/id #uuid "21608e77-ff9a-5f24-b33b-b56b7a3ed722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10795815-c959-55ee-95fa-9d0fe7de376e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120150" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/8d2588ba2b8987841a34e981aa6b7d48cdc41bebfca6c0517cc9cf67d788f591.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1b5303303f0aea22149b3d418e08724cfea9cf64943eab978fe38c0d025d73bf.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-1) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)^{2}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e030a874-8cc6-5792-93dd-b62236dccf6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349284} {:answer/id #uuid "9cef04ac-7a45-5764-8739-ed73f42f48fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=4y-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349285} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a0547a-86f5-5a90-b1a8-205d0090b09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349286} {:answer/id #uuid "e7984484-a896-5166-b786-0ae945f1eb11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349287} {:answer/id #uuid "f5351c0e-1abd-5fa8-ad15-532159bd4109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7a0547a-86f5-5a90-b1a8-205d0090b09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349286} :answer-field/id #uuid "09d21b21-7972-54f5-8395-7a49ffd9202d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b5e100b-a820-51ba-91b9-bf3f389b5600" :question/math-academy-id "q-120174" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/e0da4ce40e23a44ddfd946b9a9bf00882ca0a83db8379c6492b58355772c6006.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/ddac2bab6fb72979e1142179dfb11792cc50044de545131139edfdeff9215e3c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=\\pm 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4=(y+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454c75b7-c5f5-57e8-932c-689b569fe715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349291} {:answer/id #uuid "378fb4d2-1177-5caa-afa3-7db9da8efa33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y-1}{{y}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349292} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2f9bdb-0919-5c4e-ad96-881fb36db9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349293} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0b36f5-5776-50de-be43-34fc17a05d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=y({y}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349294} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fe1fac-21c6-5117-8565-c74b149be402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c2f9bdb-0919-5c4e-ad96-881fb36db9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349293} :answer-field/id #uuid "1031247c-3ada-554e-8663-53ce0189cb7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c80401aa-a1e2-5ade-89cc-25d35cd9468c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120196" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(y+1)(y-1)} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(1+y)(1-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.$"}]}
